15. Multi‑Agent Computational Meditation

For a society of agents, each may meditate independently and then exchange verified memories. An agent accepts a peer’s consolidated memory only if its local verification passes. Theorem C guarantees bounded network risk under the contraction condition. This provides a principled basis for collective knowledge sharing.

---

16. Engineering Principle

The principal engineering rule is: Do not maximize reasoning time; maximize expected decision improvement per unit of computation. This converts computational meditation from a metaphor into an optimization problem with a clear objective.

---

17. Core Contribution

The contribution is not the claim “LLMs meditate.” It is the proposal that artificial agents can be equipped with an adaptive computational regime functionally analogous to reflection and consolidation, whose activation and duration are determined by uncertainty, risk, and cost. The central mathematical object is

T^*(X) = \arg\max_T \left[ V(\mathcal{M}_T(X)) - V(X) - \lambda C(T) \right],

with the key structural results:

\boxed{\text{Meditation is rational iff } U(X) > \theta^*, \quad \text{with } T^* \text{ non‑decreasing in } U}

\boxed{\mathcal{R}(M_{t+k}) \le \gamma^k \mathcal{R}(M_t) + \frac{\varepsilon_V + \epsilon_M}{1-\gamma}}

\boxed{\limsup_{k\to\infty} \|\mathbf{R}_{t+k}\|_\infty \le \frac{\max_i (\varepsilon_{V,i} + \epsilon_{M,i})}{1 - \gamma_{\max}}}

---

18. Failure Modes and Limitations

CM can fail by over‑meditation, verification failure, consolidation collapse, or trigger miscalibration. The theoretical results guarantee near‑optimal allocation only under the stated assumptions; if reflection never helps (A2 fails) or the verifier error cannot be bounded (A5 fails), the framework may not improve performance. The value function  V(X)  must be estimated, and estimation bias can undermine the guarantees. Experiments should test for these failure modes explicitly.

---

19. Falsification Conditions

The framework makes specific, falsifiable predictions:

· Meditative Consolidation Hypothesis is falsified if CM agents show no improvement over baseline at equal compute, or if  \eta_{CM} \le 0 .
· Trigger function approach is falsified if random triggering performs as well as adaptive triggering.
· Threshold theorem is falsified if  T^*(X)  does not increase with  U(X)  (for interior optima).
· Memory‑safety contraction is falsified if verifier false‑negative rate  \gamma \ge 1  or if risk does not decrease after repeated verified consolidation.

---

20. Conclusion

We have presented a mathematically tightened framework for Computational Meditation as an adaptive hybrid‑control regime for allocating offline cognition in language agents. The central contributions are the Adaptive Meditation Threshold Theorem, the Memory‑Safety Contraction Theorems (single‑agent and network), and a correspondence with Expected Free Energy under specific assumptions. The framework is intentionally falsifiable and does not rely on any claim of subjective experience. We invite empirical testing and further theoretical refinement.

The resulting agent does not need to be conscious, tired, or biologically alive. It simply needs to know when acting immediately is inferior to spending computation on becoming better prepared to act later.

