The framework makes specific predictions that can be empirically tested. Each prediction is stated with the condition under which it would be falsified.

J.1 Meditative Consolidation Hypothesis

Prediction: CM agents outperform baseline agents at equal total compute on information streams with high interference and noise.

Falsified if:

· CM performance ≤ baseline performance at equal compute;
·  \eta_{CM} \le 0 ;
· Improvement occurs only through increased compute with no efficiency gain.

J.2 Adaptive Trigger Function

Prediction: Adaptive triggering outperforms random triggering and fixed schedules.

Falsified if:

· Random triggering performs as well as adaptive triggering;
· The optimal policy is always/never meditate.

J.3 Threshold Theorem

Prediction: Optimal meditation duration is non‑decreasing in epistemic uncertainty.

Falsified if:

·  T^*  does not increase with  U(X)  for interior optima;
· Meditation is rational for  U < \theta^*  or irrational for  U > \theta^* .

J.4 Memory‑Safety Contraction

Prediction: Verified consolidation reduces memory‑induced decision risk geometrically.

Falsified if:

· Verifier reduction factor  \gamma \ge 1 ;
· Risk does not decrease after repeated verified consolidation.

J.5 Network Contraction

Prediction: Peer verification prevents unbounded amplification of memory risk.

Falsified if:

· Network risk grows without bound despite local verification;
·  \gamma_{\max} \ge 1 .

